//? Title: Memoized PEG parsing: actual choices and rule reuse
//? Subtitle: Original balanced strings, real memo hits and limits of a named-rule cache
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Start with an actual repeated rule/position request, then retain the original
balanced-string grammar, negative lookahead and input-length study. Canonical
events expose accepted choices and cached successes/failures, while a
repeated-suffix control shows why this interpreter does not promise general
linear-time packrat performance.
)

\toc

> Recognition, ordered choice and whole-input acceptance

A parsing expression grammar recognizes literal bytes, named rules, sequences,
first-success ordered choices, greedy repetition, optional children and
nonconsuming predicates. A successful choice commits to that child. A later
sequence failure does not go back inside that accepted choice to try another
child. A positive predicate succeeds at the original position when its child
matches; a negative predicate succeeds there when its child fails.

The interpreter memoizes completed named(rule,position) results, including
failures. A successful result stores its end position; a failed result has an
explicit failure flag rather than a narrowed signed byte index. Matching a
prefix alone is insufficient: the public parse result accepts only when the
start rule reaches the entire input’s end. Empty literals/sequences are
nullable, and repetitions stop when their child makes no progress.

> A real memo hit after one alternative fails

Use S←A 'b'/A 'c',A←'a'+ on input aaac. The first alternative evaluates A at
position0 and reaches3, then its b fails. The second alternative requests A at
the same position and receives the completed cached success before matching c.
Exactly two named-rule bodies are evaluated(S andA), with one real memo hit. The
diagram and table select actual emitted records, rather than simulating the
parser in the report.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/packrat_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::PackratParser parser(dm::algorithms::packrat_reuse_rules(),
                                         "S");
    auto t = parser.parse_trace("aaac");
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("first", {0, 0}, "Actual A at0\nbody evaluated, end3");
    for (auto &e : t.events)
        if (e.kind == dm::algorithms::PackratEventKind::memo_hit)
            d.node("cached", {8, 0},
                   std::format("Actual memo {} at{}\nmatched {} end{}", e.rule,
                               e.position, e.matched, e.end));
    d.node("result", {16, 0},
           std::format("Actual full acceptance {}\nrule bodies {} memo hits {}",
                       t.run.accepted, t.run.rule_evaluations, t.run.memo_hits));
    d.edge("first", "cached").edge("cached", "result");
    std::print("| actual event | rule | byte position | matched | end | alternative |\n| --- | --- | ---: | " "--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &e : t.events) {
        std::string name;
        if (e.kind == dm::algorithms::PackratEventKind::rule_exit)
            name = "rule exit";
        else if (e.kind == dm::algorithms::PackratEventKind::memo_hit)
            name = "memo hit";
        else if (e.kind == dm::algorithms::PackratEventKind::choice_accept)
            name = "choice commit";
        else
            continue;
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", name, e.rule,
                   e.position, e.matched, e.end, e.alternative);
    }
    d.save_svg("build/packrat_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual event | rule | byte position | matched | end | alternative |
| --- | --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |
| rule exit | A | 0 | true | 3 | 0 |
| memo hit | A | 0 | true | 3 | 0 |
| choice commit | S | 0 | true | 4 | 1 |
| rule exit | S | 0 | true | 4 | 0 |
\image(figures/packrat_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual completed rule, cache-hit and committed-choice observations for aaac.)
\alttext(The second request for A at byte0 is served from the cache after the first outer alternative fails; full acceptance follows the c branch.)

> Retain the original balanced-string and negative-predicate examples

The original grammar S←'a'S'b'/'a''b' accepts a^n b^n for n≥1. It rejects empty
input and unbalanced strings. The original cases ab,aabb,aaabbb,aab,abb are
retained. The second original grammar Start←'a'+ !'c' accepts aaa, rejects aaac
and rejects empty input under whole-string recognition.

The original length study runs n=1,...,8. Its named-rule body count grows
linearly, but the actual memo-hit count is zero: this particular grammar never
requests a completed identical named(rule,position) again. It therefore does not
demonstrate that caching itself causes that observed growth rate. Both actual
rule and structural expression counters are retained below.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/packrat_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::PackratParser balanced(dm::algorithms::packrat_anbn_rules(),
                                           "S"), negative(
        dm::algorithms::packrat_negative_lookahead_rules(), "Start");
    std::print("| original grammar | original input | actual whole-input acceptance | actual rule bodies | " "actual memo hits |\n| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const std::string &input : {"ab", "aabb", "aaabbb", "aab", "abb"}) {
        bool ok = balanced.parse(input);
        std::print("| balanced | `{}` | {} | {} | {} |\n", input, ok,
                   balanced.rule_calls(), balanced.memo_hits());
    }
    for (const std::string &input : {"aaa", "aaac", ""}) {
        bool ok = negative.parse(input);
        std::print("| negative predicate | `{}` | {} | {} | {} |\n",
                   input.empty() ? "(empty)" : input, ok, negative.rule_calls(),
                   negative.memo_hits());
    }
    std::vector<double> x, calls;
    std::print("\n| original block length n | input bytes | actual rule bodies | actual expression visits | " "actual memo hits |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t n = 1; n <= 8; ++n) {
        auto input = dm::algorithms::packrat_balanced_input(n);
        balanced.parse(input);
        auto r = balanced.last_run();
        x.push_back(input.size());
        calls.push_back(r.rule_evaluations);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, input.size(),
                   r.rule_evaluations, r.expression_evaluations, r.memo_hits);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.lines(x, calls, "actual rule bodies", dm::graphics::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 255}).points(x, calls, dm::graphics::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 255}, 3.);
    p.title("Original balanced grammar: actual named-rule evaluations").x_label(
        "original input byte length").y_label("actual named-rule body evaluations").save_svg("build/packrat_original.svg");
}
```
