> Why this named-rule cache has no general linear guarantee

[Ford’s PEG paper|https://bford.info/pub/lang/peg/] describes the recognition
model and linear-time parser construction. This implementation memoizes named
rules only and uses ordered std::map lookup. It does not memoize every
structural expression evaluation. Thus O(R(N+1)) possible named keys, for R
rules and N bytes, does not itself bound the work spent computing each key. Map
lookup also has logarithmic comparison cost, with rule-name byte comparisons
included.

The native control S←(&A 'a')* !'a',A←'a'* accepts a string of a bytes. At every
successive position, lookahead invokes A, which greedily scans the entire
remaining suffix. These are different named keys, so the total suffix work is
quadratic even though the named-rule count is linear. The actual expression
visits below expose that distinction. This is a documented limitation of this
interpreter, not a claim that efficient general packrat parsing is impossible.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/packrat_limits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::PackratParser parser(dm::algorithms::packrat_quadratic_rules(),
                                         "S");
    std::vector<double> x, rules, expressions;
    std::print("| input a bytes | actual accepted | actual named-rule bodies | actual expression visits | " "actual memo hits | actual max depth |\n| ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t n : {4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128}) {
        bool accepted = parser.parse(std::string(n, 'a'));
        auto r = parser.last_run();
        x.push_back(std::log2(double(n)));
        rules.push_back(std::log2(double(r.rule_evaluations)));
        expressions.push_back(std::log2(double(r.expression_evaluations)));
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, accepted,
                   r.rule_evaluations, r.expression_evaluations, r.memo_hits,
                   r.max_depth);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.lines(x, rules, "named-rule bodies", dm::graphics::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 255}).points(x, rules, dm::graphics::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 255}, 3.).lines(x, expressions, "expression visits", dm::graphics::RGBA{220, 38, 38, 255}).points(x, expressions, dm::graphics::RGBA{220, 38, 38, 255}, 3.);
    p.title("Named-rule memoization: actual repeated suffix work").x_label(
        "log2(input byte length)").y_label("log2(actual selected operation count)").save_svg("build/packrat_limits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| input a bytes | actual accepted | actual named-rule bodies | actual expression visits | actual memo hits | actual max depth |
| ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 4 | true | 6 | 44 | 0 | 9 |
| 8 | true | 10 | 94 | 0 | 9 |
| 16 | true | 18 | 242 | 0 | 9 |
| 32 | true | 34 | 730 | 0 | 9 |
| 64 | true | 66 | 2474 | 0 | 9 |
| 128 | true | 130 | 9034 | 0 | 9 |
\image(figures/packrat_limits.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual linear named-rule counts versus repeated structural suffix-scan work.)
\alttext(Both curves are measured native operation counts on the same completed parses; expression visits expose the quadratic work hidden by named-rule counts alone.)

> Ordered commitment, byte ownership and resource limits

For S←('a'/'ab')'c', input abc is rejected: the choice accepts a, and its
enclosing sequence does not reconsider ab after c fails. Reversing the two
alternatives accepts abc. A literal ab matches a prefix of abc, but the
whole-input result remains false. These controls distinguish actual PEG
semantics from unrestricted backtracking or substring search.

Parser construction validates the entire owned grammar, including unreachable
references and every node’s arity/text/operator. Parsing copies and owns the
complete byte input, supports NUL/high bytes, and clears old memo/counters per
attempt. There is no retained pointer to a caller string. Direct or indirect
same-key left recursion is rejected when an active named(rule,position) is
encountered; this interpreter has no left-recursive seed-growing extension.
Nullable repetition stops on no progress.

Default policies cap input bytes1000000, grammar nodes1000000, actual named-rule
evaluations1000000, expression visits5000000, combined rule/expression depth512
and trace units1000000. Zero disables each optional policy, without making the
native stack unbounded. Legacy long count accessors are separately guarded
against overflow. An exception clears incomplete memo, leaves completed=false
and explicit partial last-attempt counters, and returns no partial trace; a
later call starts fresh.

Trace quota counts input bytesN+three decision fields and each actual
event/final memo record as1+its rule-name bytes. Grammar, working
cache/capacities and fixed counter fields are excluded. Admission precedes
retained input/event/memo copies. Copies own grammar/input/cache, and concurrent
mutation of a single parser needs caller synchronization. Time/storage depend on
actual expressions, keys and history rather than an unqualified linear bound.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/packrat_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    using namespace dm::algorithms;
    std::vector<std::string> labels{"prefix only", "short choice first",
                                    "long choice first", "nullable repetition",
                                    "embedded NUL"};
    std::vector<PackratRun> runs;
    auto check = [&](PackratParser parser, const std::string & input) {
        parser.parse(input);
        runs.push_back(parser.last_run());
    };
    check(PackratParser({{"S", peg_lit("ab")}}, "S"), "abc");
    check(PackratParser({{"S",
                          peg_seq({peg_choice({peg_lit("a"), peg_lit("ab")}),
                                   peg_lit("c")})}}, "S"), "abc");
    check(PackratParser({{"S",
                          peg_seq({peg_choice({peg_lit("ab"), peg_lit("a")}),
                                   peg_lit("c")})}}, "S"), "abc");
    check(PackratParser({{"S", peg_star(peg_lit(""))}}, "S"), "");
    std::string bytes("a\0b", 3);
    check(PackratParser({{"S", peg_lit(bytes)}}, "S"), bytes);
    std::vector<double> accepted;
    std::print("| explicit grammar/input control | actual prefix match | actual end byte | actual " "whole-input acceptance | actual expression visits |\n| --- | --- | ---: | --- | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < runs.size(); ++i) {
        auto &r = runs[i];
        accepted.push_back(r.accepted);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", labels[i], r.matched_prefix,
                   r.end, r.accepted, r.expression_evaluations);
    }
    auto p =
        dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(accepted, labels, "actual acceptance");
    p.title("Byte PEG controls: whole input and ordered commitment").x_label(
        "explicit validated grammar/input").y_label("actual whole-input acceptance").y_limits(0, 1.2).save_svg("build/packrat_controls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| explicit grammar/input control | actual prefix match | actual end byte | actual whole-input acceptance | actual expression visits |
| --- | --- | ---: | --- | ---: |
| prefix only | true | 2 | false | 1 |
| short choice first | false | 0 | false | 4 |
| long choice first | true | 3 | true | 4 |
| nullable repetition | true | 0 | true | 2 |
| embedded NUL | true | 3 | true | 1 |
\image(figures/packrat_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual prefix-versus-whole recognition, choice order, nullable repetition and embedded-NUL controls.)
\alttext(A successful prefix alone does not accept the whole string; literal and input ownership remain byte-exact even when the string contains NUL.)

> Verification

This ten-report batch passes 50 CTest cases, 25 directly instrumented ASan/UBSan
cases, 174 compiled and executed public examples, 707/707 scoped documentation
definitions and ten representative clangd hovers. Independent Python checks
verify 5662 records with 27916 checks: optimal prefix-code costs, train-only
Titanic category counts and held-out confusion, and all 5001 actual original
Lorenz RK4 steps. Frozen valid-input comparisons cover all ten producers, with
intentional guard, ownership, arithmetic and endpoint corrections stated
separately. Twelve checkout headers and twelve byte-identical installed
development headers compile independently; an installed-header native consumer
links and runs. All 40 complete programs execute, and 40 native figures have
been visually reviewed. HTML/Markdown exports preserve computed results and
image bytes. These are scoped native checks. Receipts:
